4.  COMMERCIAL USE AND REVENUE SHARE

Commercial use is permitted only on the terms of this Section.

Where the Software, or a derivative containing a substantial part of it,
is used to generate revenue, is incorporated into a commercial product,
or is used to provide a paid service other than a prohibited hosted
service, the user must pay the Rights Holder a royalty equal to five
percent (5%) of Net Attributable Revenue.

“Net Attributable Revenue” means amounts actually received by the user
from unaffiliated customers that are directly attributable to the
commercial product, service or activity using the Software, excluding
VAT and similar sales taxes, refunds, chargebacks and separately
itemized pass-through expenses paid to third parties. If the Software is
only one component of a wider product or service, revenue must be
allocated on a reasonable, consistent and documented basis to the part
attributable to the Software.

No royalty is due for: a. personal use; b. internal organizational use
that is not separately commercialized; c. non-commercial forks,
modifications and public source-code publication permitted by Section 3.

The user must keep accurate records of Net Attributable Revenue, provide
a quarterly statement within thirty (30) days after each calendar
quarter, and pay the corresponding royalty within thirty (30) days after
sending the statement. The Rights Holder may, on reasonable prior notice
and no more than once per calendar year, appoint an independent auditor
bound by confidentiality to verify the relevant records. If an audit
identifies an underpayment of more than five percent (5%) for the
audited period, the user must reimburse reasonable audit costs in
addition to the unpaid royalties.

The five percent (5%) royalty is the default contractual rate under this
license. A separate written agreement signed by the Rights Holder and
the user may specify a different rate, reporting method or commercial
scope.

5.  PROHIBITED USES

Unless the Rights Holder has signed a separate written amendment
expressly authorizing the specific activity, you must not:

a.  sell, resell, rent, lease, sublicense or commercially distribute
    copies of the Software, in source or object-code form, as a
    standalone product;
b.  sell, publish or distribute the Software or a substantial portion of
    its source code as a paid download, package, template, module or
    competing code product;
c.  offer the Software, its functionality, or a material derivative as
    SaaS, PaaS, an API service, hosted application, managed service or
    other network-accessible service to third parties, whether paid or
    free;
d.  provide third parties with a copy of the Software or operational
    access to an instance of the Software, except for the permitted
    publication of a non-commercial source-code fork under Sections 3(e)
    and 6;
e.  publish or distribute a fork for commercial purposes without written
    authorization, even if the fork has been modified;
f.  remove, obscure or misrepresent copyright, authorship, attribution
    or license notices;
g.  claim ownership of the original Software or represent it as your own
    independently created product.

For clarity, commercial use of the Software within a product or service
that you operate yourself is permitted subject to the royalty in Section
4, but SaaS and other hosted third-party services remain prohibited
unless expressly authorized by a separate written amendment.

6.  FORKS, MODIFICATIONS AND REDISTRIBUTION

You may create, modify and publish forks free of charge for
non-commercial purposes, subject to this license.

When redistributing a fork or modified version, you must: a. include a
complete copy of this license; b. retain all existing copyright and
attribution notices; c. clearly identify your modifications and their
dates, where reasonably practicable; d. not imply that the Rights Holder
endorses your fork; e. not impose additional terms that remove or limit
the permissions granted by this license for the original Software.

A fork does not acquire broader rights than the original Software.
Commercial fork distribution remains prohibited without a separate
written amendment. Commercial use of a fork within the user’s own
product or service is subject to the royalty in Section 4 and the SaaS
restriction in Section 5.

